1
00:06:23,076 --> 00:06:29,076
When his world was stolen, Mark Hogancamp made a world of his own.

2
00:12:46,153 --> 00:12:52,153
Marwencol is a documentary film directed by Jeff Malmberg.

3
00:19:09,230 --> 00:19:15,230
After a vicious attack leaves him brain-damaged and broke, Mark Hogancamp seeks recovery in
"Marwencol", a 1/6th scale World War II-era town he creates in his backyard.

4
00:25:32,307 --> 00:25:38,307
Self, played by Mark Hogancamp.

5
00:31:55,384 --> 00:32:01,384
Self, played by Emmanuel Nneji.

6
00:38:18,461 --> 00:38:24,461
Self, played by Edda Hogancamp.

7
00:44:41,538 --> 00:44:47,538
Self, played by Tom Neubauer.

8
00:51:04,615 --> 00:51:10,615
Self, played by Julie Swarthout.

9
00:57:27,692 --> 00:57:33,692
Self, played by Janet Wikane.

10
01:03:50,769 --> 01:03:56,769
Marwencol (also known as Village of the Dolls in the UK) is a 2010 American documentary film
that explores the life and work of artist and photographer Mark Hogancamp.

11
01:10:13,846 --> 01:10:19,846
It is the debut feature of director Jeff Malmberg, produced
through his production company Open Face.

12
01:16:36,923 --> 01:16:42,923
It was the inspiration for Welcome to Marwen, a 2018
drama directed by Robert Zemeckis.
